<section xml:id="Exam1-Review" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Exam 1 topics</title>

<p>
    <alert> Section 11.1 Definition of a ring </alert>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Definitions and concepts: 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    ring, subring, unit, field, integral domain
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Be able to: 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Prove that a given set is a ring or subring
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Reason using the properties of a ring as in the proof of Proposition 11.1.5
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
    <alert> Section 11.2 Polynomial rings </alert>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Definitions and concepts: 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                      polynomial ring of one variable, polynomial ring of multiple variables  
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                       degree, leading coefficient, monic polynomial  
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        addition and multiplication of polynomials
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Results to know
            </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Proposition 11.2.9 (Division with Remainder) and the corrolaries
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Be able to: 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Prove some basic results about polynomials, e.g.: If <m>R</m> is an integral domain then <m> R[x]</m> is an integral domain, but if <m>F</m> is a field, then <m>F[x]</m> is never a field. 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>

</p>


<p>
    <alert> Section 11.3 Homomorphisms and ideals </alert>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Definition and concepts:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    homomorphism, isomorphism, kernel, characteristic
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    the notation <m>I+J</m> and <m>IJ</m> where <m>I</m>, <m>J</m> are ideals
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    principal ideal, ideal <m>(a_1, \dots, n_n)</m> generated by <m>a_1, \dots, a_n</m>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Results to know: 
        </p>
 <ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Proposition 11.3.4 (Substitution principle)
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Proposition 11.3.19 <alert> -- be able to prove </alert>
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Proposition 11.3.21
        </p>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Proposition 11.3.22 <alert> -- be able to prove </alert> 
        </p>
    </li>
 </ul>
    </li>
<li>
    <p>
        Be able to
    </p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Prove that a given set is an ideal
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Prove that a given function is a ring homomorphism 
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Compute the kernel of a homomorphism: see Example 11.3.16(a) and Example 11.3.23 and homework problems
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Use the substitution principle to construct homomorphisms
            </p>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Various resoning involving the concepts (prove or disprove equalities/containments of ideals)
            </p>
        </li>
    </ul>
</li>

</ul>

</p>

<p>
    <alert> Section 11.4 Quotient rings</alert>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Definitions and concepts:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    coset, quotient ring, the notation <m>\overline{a}</m> for cosets
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    addition, multiplication in the coset ring
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    <m>\overline{a}=\overline{b} </m> in <m>R/I</m> <m>\iff</m> <m>a-b\in I</m>.
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Results to know: 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Theorem 11.4.2 Mapping property of quotient rings (including FIT) <alert> -- be able to prove </alert> 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Correspondence theorem, concentrating mostly on quotient rings (what do the ideal of a quotient ring look like?). See what I referred to as SIT (Second Isomorphism Theorem) in class. 
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Be able to 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use FIT and SIT to prove isomorphisms of quotient rings, as in the homework. 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use the Correspondence theorem to describe ideals of quotient rings, see Example 11.4.4(b)
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
    <alert> Section 11.5 Adjoining elements</alert>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Definition and concepts:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The ring <m>R[\alpha]</m> obtained by adjoining to <m>R</m> and element <m>\alpha</m> satisfying <m>f(\alpha)=0</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Results to know: 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Proposition 11.5.5 <alert> -- be able to prove (a)</alert> 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Be able to:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Use the methods from the previous section to compute various rings obtained by adjoining one or more elements, as in the homework. 
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                         Use Proposition 11.5.5 to express elements in <m>R[\alpha]</m> in short form as indicated by Proposition 11.5.5, and to derive other conclusions.  For example, can you say how many elements are in the ring <m>\mathbb  Z_3[\alpha]=\mathbb Z_3[x]/(x^3+2x+1)</m>? 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>

<p>
    <alert>Section 11.6 - Product rings</alert>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Definitions and concepts: 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    product ring, idempotent
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Results to know
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                Proposition 11.6.2
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The Chinese Remainder Theorem (See hw): If <m>I+J=R</m>, then <m>I\cap J=IJ</m> and <m> R/IJ\cong R/I\times R/J</m> <alert> -- be able to prove </alert>
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Be able to
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Prove isomorphisms and other results involving products
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Apply the Chinese Remainder Theorem in problems about polynomial rings
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>
</p>

<p> <alert> Section 1.7 Fractions </alert>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Definitions and concepts:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Fractions as equivalence classes, the fraction field <m>Q(R)</m> of an integral domain <m>R</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        addition and multiplication of fractions
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li> 
            <p>Results to know: 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Theorem 11.7.2(a) <alert>-- be able to prove</alert>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Be able to:
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Prove that addition and multiplication of fractions is well-defined
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>

    </ul>
</p>

<p>
    <alert>Section 11.7 - Maximal ideals</alert>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <p>
                Definitions and concepts: 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        maximal ideal, irreducible polynomial
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Results to know: 
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Proposition 11.8.2
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Proposition 11.8.3 <alert>-- be able to prove</alert>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Proposition 11.8.4, Corollary 11.8.5
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        A polynomial of degree 2 or 3 is irreducible in <m>F[x]</m> with <m>F</m> a field, if and only if it has no roots in <m>F</m>. 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <p>
                Be able to
            </p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Find the maximal ideals of various rings, using the results of this sections and maybe the Correspondence Theorem, or other reasoning  (see the hw). 
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </li>
    </ul>
</p>

