<p>
<alert>Section 14.1 - Modules</alert>
<ul>
    <li>
        <p>
            Definitions and concepts:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    module, submodule, homomorphism (of modules), isomorphism, quotient module
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    The notation <m>R^n</m> 
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Results to know: 
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Propostion 14.1.3, Theorem 14.1.6
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                Abelian group and <m>\mathbb Z</m>-module are equivalent concepts (in which sense?)
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
    <li>
        <p>
            Be able to:
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Show a given set is a module, submodule. 
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Show a given function is a module homomorphism
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <p>
                    Use FIT and SIT to show isomorphisms of modules
                </p>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </li>
</ul>
</p>

<p>
